# Nineteen defences in cover.py and one in this suite's own fixture, twenty
# mutations, each red on its own test.
# Run with scripts in the session scratchpad: one exact string swap at a time,
# the pattern asserted to hit exactly once, the file restored afterwards and the
# restoration asserted.
#   python3 -m unittest <the tests named below>   (cwd scripts/avatar)
#
# RE-RUN 2026-09-10, twice. First after the served body was replaced by its
# trimmed self and this suite's fixture moved to the pre-cull blob in git
# (cover_test.py names it): pointed at the trimmed body these tests trim a body
# with nothing left to cut, and every one of these mutations goes green, so the
# fixture is the whole thing. Then again after `covered` learned which skin
# layers are inner wear, which is M17 to M20 below.
#
# M26 to M30 were added on the round-3 code review and run with a second copy of
# the runner (mutate4.py) that clears __pycache__ around each swap. The first
# batch reported M26, M27 and M28 all red on M26's test: three of these swaps
# land in the same second at the same source length, and CPython keys its cached
# bytecode on (mtime, size), so two of the three ran the previous mutation's
# code. The result looked like a pass, three reds for three mutations, and was a
# stale-bytecode artifact. Any batch that mutates one file more than once needs
# the cache cleared between swaps.

