# M17 and M19 both land on the head camera because they are the two ways of
# losing the same thing. M17 stops asking what the ray hits first, so a mouth
# vertex reads the hood behind the head; M19 declares the outer body to be inner
# wear, which exempts it from the same rule. Either way the collar takes the
# neck, and neither shows on the full-body framings the rest of this work uses.
#
# M18 and M20 are the other direction, and they are separate defences: M18 is
# the threshold that decides which layers are inner wear, M20 is whether the
# looser rule is applied to the layers it names. Removing either leaves the
# camisole under a rule that will not cut it, and the cardigan reads 171 pixels
# of 150 on the ten-clip gate.
#
# M21 is the third condition on the same decision and it fails silently on this
# body, which is why it needs its own mutation rather than a sentence. Dropping
# the requirement that an inner-wear layer be body-sized lets the eyebrows, the
# eyeliner and the eye highlight in -- they paint over 19.2%, 46.7% and 7.7% of
# themselves, under the transparency threshold on their own. On THIS body the
# cut does not change by one triangle, because nothing on the face has a garment
# within 150mm; on a dress-up export with the hood up it deletes the eyebrows,
# and both shipping guards are blind to that (cover.holes sees face skin still
# painting behind them; pierce.py only counts skin coming through cloth). The
# test reads the classification directly for exactly that reason.
#
# ON THE ONE THAT NEEDED ITS TEST CHANGED FIRST. M6 was pointed at
# `test_it_converges`, which the run below shows passing either way: with the
# bounding box the loop still converges in two rounds with no holes. What the
# box changes is the cut, and `test_the_hoodie_stops_showing_the_body` is what
# reads it. Measured on the export over the same two poses, 2026-09-10:
#
#   containment   330 triangles restored, 234 left in each inner layer,
#                 cardigan 48 at the failing pose, 0 holes, converged in 2
#   bounding box  729 triangles restored, 366 left in each inner layer,
#                 cardigan 174 at the failing pose, 0 holes, converged in 2
#
# A test that watched convergence would have gone on passing through both.
#
# M24 is a cost, not a correctness bug, which is why it took a reviewer to find
# it and why it gets a test rather than a note. glb.rebuild lays down every
# bufferView whether or not an accessor points at it, so appending a fresh index
# accessor and leaving the old one keeps the deleted triangles in the file: the
# culled body came out 101,004 bytes LARGER than the export it was cut from,
# with 243,732 bytes of indices nothing draws. Rewriting each accessor in place
# makes it 143,284 bytes smaller instead, and the index arrays are byte-identical
# to the version that appended, so nothing on screen moved.

