# M26 to M29 are four conditions on the decision M24 introduced, and only the
# first of them existed before the round-3 code review. `_rewrite_indices`
# replaces the accessor's WHOLE bufferView starting at byte zero, and the check
# in front of it counted readers of the ACCESSOR. glTF allows three layouts that
# pass that count and lose data anyway: an index accessor with no bufferView at
# all, one that starts partway into its view (the new indices go in at zero and
# it still reads from its own offset), and a view a second accessor shares,
# whose bytes are dropped. Neither the 2026-09-09 export nor the body that ships
# has any of the three, every index accessor owning its view at offset zero, so
# these change nothing about what shipped. That is asserted rather than assumed
# by test_the_bodies_that_go_through_this_module_take_the_cheap_path, so the
# extra conditions cannot quietly send the whole export down the appending path
# and grow the file again. They exist because dressup.py is the entry for a body
# exported by a tool this repo does not control.
#
# M30 is a blind spot in this suite rather than a defence in cover.py. The face
# test selects the vertices above the collar with a hardcoded world height,
# `pos[:, 1] > 1.45`, and asserts none of them is covered. On a shorter body, or
# after the face primitive is renumbered, that selection is EMPTY and the
# assertion passes on nothing. 653 of this primitive's 2,266 vertices are above
# 1.45m here, so the test now asserts the selection is non-empty first. The
# mutation raises the height to 10.45m, which is the shape of that failure.

M32 the view count walks only the accessor list, missing sparse blocks and images: exit 1, tests ran=True, 1 red
    FAIL: test_a_view_a_sparse_block_reads_is_not_overwritten

# M32 is the round-4 code review's finding on M29, and it is the same class of
# mistake M29 itself was: a check that answers a narrower question than the
# sentence above it claims. `_in_place_safe` said "no second accessor shares
# the view" and counted only `accessor['bufferView']`. A bufferView can be read
# without any accessor naming it at top level -- a sparse accessor's index and
# value blocks each point at one, and an embedded image is one -- so a morph
# target whose sparse values sat in the same view was overwritten with the new
# indices, silently, and the deltas came back as denormal floats. The reviewer
# built that input and ran it. Neither shipped body has the layout; `_view_users`
# is the fix and M32 is what holds it.
#
# M26 to M29 were re-run after this change, because the condition they mutate
# moved into `_view_users`. Each is still red on its own test; M29 now takes the
# sparse test with it, which is correct -- returning True there disables the
# view check for both shapes.
